    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:2em;">Use it as the second layer of your school-adjacent property search:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Confirm the 12 catchments that matter to your family.</li>
      <li>Open the PDF and note the developments falling inside each 2&nbsp;km ring.</li>
      <li>Overlay your own filters &mdash; tenure, size, floor level, PSF budget &mdash; against the widened list, rather than restricting yourself artificially to the sub-1&nbsp;km subset.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>If you'd like a project-by-project PrimeKey Analysis (yield, tenure, GLS pipeline, MOP cluster and school-catchment scoring) laid over your shortlist, our <a href="/navis-primekey-analysis">NAVIS PrimeKey Analysis</a> is exactly the tool for that stage &mdash; and every planning conversation begins inside the <a href="/property-wealth-planning">Property Wealth Planning framework</a> so the primary-school decision sits inside the wider portfolio picture, not on its own.</p>

    <h2>The bottom line</h2>

    <p>The 2027 P1 Registration Framework does two useful things: it expands the open-registration pool at most schools, and at 12 popular schools it flattens the distance hierarchy inside the 2&nbsp;km ring while protecting the local half of Phase 2C. For parents, it's a materially fairer shot at these schools without the mandatory sub-1&nbsp;km real-estate purchase. For buyers, it re-prices the marginal cost of the school premium &mdash; the 2&nbsp;km ring is now the meaningful boundary at these 12 catchments, not the sub-1&nbsp;km one.</p>

    <p>Neither of those changes should be read as &ldquo;the catchments no longer matter.&rdquo; They do, and they will continue to shape both P1 outcomes and long-run resale demand. What has changed is <em>where</em> inside the ring the premium sits, and how brutally it's priced. The right response, for buyers who care about a specific catchment, is to widen the search and re-evaluate on merit.</p>

